    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d122">

                <pb facs="0235" n="133" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg133"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5799: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-01-26T11:00:00+08:00">January 26, 1933—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 26—1:32 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>92. Following from American Consul General at Mukden: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“January 25, noon. All available information indicates that drive on
                            Jehol has not been canceled but that preparations therefor are not yet
                            completed. February or early part of March is regarded <pb facs="0236"
                                n="134" xml:id="pg134"/> in well-informed circles as the best season
                            for the drive. It is understood that major disposition of troops at
                            vantage points has already taken place.</p>
                        <p>Seven heavy bombers bombarded volunteers in the vicinity of Kailu on the
                            23d. On the same day bandit clearing operations started in the Liaohsi
                            area southwest of Mukden in which Manchukuo troops reenforced with
                            Japanese units are being used.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
